 (2,2,2) first.\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/synthesis.md:151:\"Build it\" is now: commission a multi-expedition AG (or determinantal-CA) dimension-theory sub-expedition.\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/synthesis.md:165:determinantal heights; {BA=0} is reducible, codim 3 not 4), and NO regular sequence cuts it — so the cheap\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/synthesis.md:166:CA shortcut is unavailable. A from-scratch Lean determinantal-CA proof needs Schubert/determinantal ideals\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/synthesis.md:169:quiver determinantal CA.\"\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/synthesis.md:171:**Hardened conclusion:** BOTH build routes (algebraic-group orbit dimension; determinantal-CA) hit the\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/synthesis.md:206:closure by Thm 3.8 cited), `codimRep coord Z := (vanishingIdeal (coord '' Z)).height` (standard affine\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/scratch.md:21:- N=1 d=(2,2): {rank A ≤ 1} codim 1 (generic determinantal (2-1)²=1).\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/scratch.md:28:2. **Excess intersection — codim is NOT a sum of independent determinantal heights.**\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/scratch.md:54:+ rank): excess intersection, non-CI, codim = Krull-dim drop of a determinantal-type ideal. Certifying\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/scratch.md:71:  independent determinantal heights; the conditions are highly dependent (excess intersection), as my\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/scratch.md:76:  codimension proof is a SEPARATE Schubert/quiver-determinantal package; matching it to dim Ext¹ is a\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/scratch.md:77:  combinatorial homological comparison, \"not a generic determinantal-height or regular-sequence proof.\"\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/scratch.md:80:\"A from-scratch Lean proof via determinantal CA is the **larger** build\" — needs polynomial rings,\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/scratch.md:81:minors, determinantal/Schubert ideals, radical/prime/height, standard-monomial or Gröbner theory, AND\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/scratch.md:83:Lean formalization, I would not start with quiver determinantal CA.\" The algebraic-group/orbit-dimension\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/scratch.md:90:Cohen–Macaulay determinantal ideal; = Buch–Fulton rectangle sum = dim Ext¹). It is NOT computable by the\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/scratch.md:91:cheap regular-sequence/Eagon–Northcott route — it is an excess/non-CI quiver-determinantal height. As a\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/scratch.md:92:Lean build it is LARGER than the algebraic-group orbit-dimension route, not smaller. The determinantal-CA\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/scratch.md:93:route does NOT shrink Layer 2; it relocates the ocean (from AG to Schubert/quiver determinantal CA +\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-prompt.md:9:sequences, determinantal ideal heights, Eagon-Northcott, Cohen-Macaulayness) WITHOUT invoking\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-prompt.md:18:(2) Is the CODIMENSION of this rank locus in Rep_d computable by classical determinantal CA?\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-prompt.md:19:    I.e. is codim Ō_M = sum of determinantal-height contributions, via a known regular sequence /\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-prompt.md:20:    determinantal ideal height theorem, so that one could in principle PROVE codim = dim Ext^1(M,M)\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-prompt.md:21:    using only commutative-algebra height theory (Krull height, Cohen-Macaulay determinantal rings)\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-prompt.md:30:  determinantal-CA computation, or does the standard proof genuinely route through algebraic groups\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-prompt.md:33:  determinantal codim (m-r)(n-r). But the type-A orbit closure intersects MANY such loci\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-prompt.md:39:  locus by determinantal CA\". Compare to a from-scratch \"algebraic-group + orbit-dimension\" build.\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-prompt.md:46:- If the codimension does NOT decompose as a sum of independent determinantal heights (excess\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-prompt.md:48:- Do not assume Mathlib has any of: algebraic groups, orbit dimension, determinantal variety codim,\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-answer.md:36:But this is **not** a naive sum of generic determinantal codimensions for all interval products. The conditions are highly dependent. The correct theory is quiver loci / Zelevinsky maps / Schubert determinantal ideals / matrix Schubert varieties, not a regular sequence of all product minors. KMS explicitly frame these ideals as “minors in products of generic matrices” analyzed through Schubert determinantal ideals and Zelevinsky’s map. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0308142))\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-answer.md:42:No, not in general. These quiver ideals are **Cohen-Macaulay, normal, reduced/prime** in the equioriented type-A case, but they are not generally complete intersections. Their height is the Buch-Fulton/KMS rectangle sum above, not the sum of all individual interval determinantal heights. The right CA/geometric package is Schubert/quiver determinantal theory: Zelevinsky, Lakshmibai-Magyar, Buch-Fulton, Knutson-Miller-Shimozono; Bobinski-Zwara extend normality/CM/rational-singularity results to arbitrary type-A orientations. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.6261))\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-answer.md:56:Inference: in equioriented type A, one can separately compute `dim Ext¹(M,M)` from interval summands and match the KMS lace-sum formula, but that is a combinatorial homological comparison, not a generic determinantal-height or regular-sequence proof.\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-answer.md:60:A from-scratch Lean proof via “determinantal CA” is the larger build. You would need polynomial rings, minors, determinantal ideals, radical/prime/height, matrix Schubert ideals or standard monomial/Groebner theory, and dimension-height infrastructure not currently assumed.\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-answer.md:62:The algebraic-group/orbit-dimension route is still substantial, but smaller and more self-contained for proving `codim = Ext¹`: mostly finite-dimensional linear algebra, `End`, `Ext¹` via an explicit complex, rank-nullity, plus a comparatively small amount of affine orbit-dimension theory. If the goal is a Lean formalization, I would not start with quiver determinantal CA.\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:23:sequences, determinantal ideal heights, Eagon-Northcott, Cohen-Macaulayness) WITHOUT invoking\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:32:(2) Is the CODIMENSION of this rank locus in Rep_d computable by classical determinantal CA?\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:33:    I.e. is codim Ō_M = sum of determinantal-height contributions, via a known regular sequence /\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:34:    determinantal ideal height theorem, so that one could in principle PROVE codim = dim Ext^1(M,M)\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:35:    using only commutative-algebra height theory (Krull height, Cohen-Macaulay determinantal rings)\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:44:  determinantal-CA computation, or does the standard proof genuinely route through algebraic groups\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:47:  determinantal codim (m-r)(n-r). But the type-A orbit closure intersects MANY such loci\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:53:  locus by determinantal CA\". Compare to a from-scratch \"algebraic-group + orbit-dimension\" build.\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:60:- If the codimension does NOT decompose as a sum of independent determinantal heights (excess\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:62:- Do not assume Mathlib has any of: algebraic groups, orbit dimension, determinantal variety codim,\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:80:./codex/rankloc-prompt.md:18:(2) Is the CODIMENSION of this rank locus in Rep_d computable by classical determinantal CA?\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:81:./codex/rankloc-prompt.md:20:    determinantal ideal height theorem, so that one could in principle PROVE codim = dim Ext^1(M,M)\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:84:./codex/rankloc-prompt.md:33:  determinantal codim (m-r)(n-r). But the type-A orbit closure intersects MANY such loci\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:88:./codex/rankloc-run.log:32:(2) Is the CODIMENSION of this rank locus in Rep_d computable by classical determinantal CA?\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:89:./codex/rankloc-run.log:34:    determinantal ideal height theorem, so that one could in principle PROVE codim = dim Ext^1(M,M)\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:92:./codex/rankloc-run.log:47:  determinantal codim (m-r)(n-r). But the type-A orbit closure intersects MANY such loci\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:185:But this is **not** a naive sum of generic determinantal codimensions for all interval products. The conditions are highly dependent. The correct theory is quiver loci / Zelevinsky maps / Schubert determinantal ideals / matrix Schubert varieties, not a regular sequence of all product minors. KMS explicitly frame these ideals as “minors in products of generic matrices” analyzed through Schubert determinantal ideals and Zelevinsky’s map. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0308142))\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:191:No, not in general. These quiver ideals are **Cohen-Macaulay, normal, reduced/prime** in the equioriented type-A case, but they are not generally complete intersections. Their height is the Buch-Fulton/KMS rectangle sum above, not the sum of all individual interval determinantal heights. The right CA/geometric package is Schubert/quiver determinantal theory: Zelevinsky, Lakshmibai-Magyar, Buch-Fulton, Knutson-Miller-Shimozono; Bobinski-Zwara extend normality/CM/rational-singularity results to arbitrary type-A orientations. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.6261))\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:205:Inference: in equioriented type A, one can separately compute `dim Ext¹(M,M)` from interval summands and match the KMS lace-sum formula, but that is a combinatorial homological comparison, not a generic determinantal-height or regular-sequence proof.\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:209:A from-scratch Lean proof via “determinantal CA” is the larger build. You would need polynomial rings, minors, determinantal ideals, radical/prime/height, matrix Schubert ideals or standard monomial/Groebner theory, and dimension-height infrastructure not currently assumed.\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:211:The algebraic-group/orbit-dimension route is still substantial, but smaller and more self-contained for proving `codim = Ext¹`: mostly finite-dimensional linear algebra, `End`, `Ext¹` via an explicit complex, rank-nullity, plus a comparatively small amount of affine orbit-dimension theory. If the goal is a Lean formalization, I would not start with quiver determinantal CA.\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:249:But this is **not** a naive sum of generic determinantal codimensions for all interval products. The conditions are highly dependent. The correct theory is quiver loci / Zelevinsky maps / Schubert determinantal ideals / matrix Schubert varieties, not a regular sequence of all product minors. KMS explicitly frame these ideals as “minors in products of generic matrices” analyzed through Schubert determinantal ideals and Zelevinsky’s map. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0308142))\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:255:No, not in general. These quiver ideals are **Cohen-Macaulay, normal, reduced/prime** in the equioriented type-A case, but they are not generally complete intersections. Their height is the Buch-Fulton/KMS rectangle sum above, not the sum of all individual interval determinantal heights. The right CA/geometric package is Schubert/quiver determinantal theory: Zelevinsky, Lakshmibai-Magyar, Buch-Fulton, Knutson-Miller-Shimozono; Bobinski-Zwara extend normality/CM/rational-singularity results to arbitrary type-A orientations. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.6261))\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:269:Inference: in equioriented type A, one can separately compute `dim Ext¹(M,M)` from interval summands and match the KMS lace-sum formula, but that is a combinatorial homological comparison, not a generic determinantal-height or regular-sequence proof.\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:273:A from-scratch Lean proof via “determinantal CA” is the larger build. You would need polynomial rings, minors, determinantal ideals, radical/prime/height, matrix Schubert ideals or standard monomial/Groebner theory, and dimension-height infrastructure not currently assumed.\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/04-rankloc-probe/codex/rankloc-run.log:275:The algebraic-group/orbit-dimension route is still substantial, but smaller and more self-contained for proving `codim = Ext¹`: mostly finite-dimensional linear algebra, `End`, `Ext¹` via an explicit complex, rank-nullity, plus a comparatively small amount of affine orbit-dimension theory. If the goal is a Lean formalization, I would not start with quiver determinantal CA.\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-codimension/threads/06-phaseB-hybrid/codex/codimrep-answer.md:1:**VERDICT:** PREFERRED: `Ideal.height (MvPolynomial.vanishingIdeal k V) : ℕ∞`; single reason: Mathlib v4.29 has this affine vanishing-ideal/height API, but no ready Zariski topology on raw `Tuple d` / `Fin n → k`.\n./expeditions/2026-06-17-ext-co