In this section, we explain its difference for real and complex fields. Let f be a nonzero holomorphic
function over C or an analytic function over R on a smooth variety, Y , and let Z ⊂ Y be a closed
subscheme. The log canonical threshold, λZ (Y, f ), is defined analytically as:

                             λZ (Y, f ) = sup{c : |f |−c is locally L2 near Z}

over C, and:
                             λZ (Y, f ) = sup{c : |f |−c is locally L1 near Z}
over R [11,12]. It is known that if f is a polynomial or a convergent power series, then λ0 (Cd , f ) is
the largest root of the Bernstein-Sato polynomial, b(s) ∈ C[s], of f , where b(s)f s = P f s+1 for a linear
differential operator, P [29–31]. The log canonical threshold, λZ (Y, f ), also corresponds to the largest
